ISRG "Root YR", cross-signed by ISRG Root X1.

WHY THIS FILE IS IN THE REPO
----------------------------
bharatmapsparivesh.parivesh.nic.in serves only its leaf certificate and the
Let's Encrypt "YR1" intermediate. The chain then stops, at a root the client is
expected to already trust. Windows fetches the missing link itself (AIA
chasing); OpenSSL does not, so on Linux the handshake fails outright with
CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED unless the OS bundle already carries ISRG Root YR.
GitHub's ubuntu-latest runner does not, which killed the first scheduled run:

    TLS: OS trust store (truststore)
    TLS chain incomplete -- run `pip install truststore`

truststore does not help: on Linux it defers to the same OS bundle.

Note that parivesh.nic.in (the portal, used for proponent names) serves a THREE
certificate chain including this cross-sign, so it verifies anywhere. Only the
GIS host is truncated. This file is simply that third certificate, lifted from
the portal's own chain, so the GIS host can be verified the same way.

This is a public CA certificate. It grants no access and contains no secret --
it only lets a client complete a chain the server declines to serve in full.
The alternative was --insecure, which would have disabled verification against
a government host entirely. That is not a trade worth making.

MAINTENANCE
-----------
Valid until 2 Sept 2028. If it expires, or Let's Encrypt rotates roots, refresh
it with:

    openssl s_client -connect parivesh.nic.in:443 \
        -servername parivesh.nic.in -showcerts </dev/null

and take the certificate whose subject is the issuer of "YR1". The code adds
this to the system trust store rather than replacing it, so once a runner's OS
bundle carries the root on its own, this file becomes redundant rather than
wrong.

